WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:06.720
All of this kinda connects to this.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.720
We added sound.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:11.599
It's a silent movie.

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:16.179
This chart shows all the movies directed or
produced by legendary director D.W.

00:00:16.179 --> 00:00:17.750
Griffith in 1912.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.820
He was a Hollywood titan, but most of his
movies?

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.310
They were about the same length.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:27.140
Around 15 minutes, with the exceptions topping
out around a half hour long.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:33.870
Now look at the run time for 1915’s Birth
of A Nation.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:34.870
What happened there?

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:40.390
The reason movies changed from 15 minute trifles
to “featured attractions,” can be traced

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:42.379
to one silent movie.

00:00:42.379 --> 00:00:45.760
It changed the game in a way that transformed
all movies.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.290
But it wasn’t invented in Hollywood.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:57.449
It came from 6,000 miles away.

00:00:57.449 --> 00:01:01.609
This is a foot — here you go metric people.

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:05.230
Early silent film historians actually talk
about these movies in...feet.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:11.200
And it makes sense — the same way that measuring
tape comes on a reel, movies come on reels

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.200
too.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.750
One reel of film is 1,000 feet, give or take.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:19.460
That’s 15 minutes or fewer, depending on
what frame rate you play it back at.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:24.730
In the early days of movies — the 1900s
— almost all movies were about 1,000 feet.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:25.960
One reel.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.740
15 minutes or fewer.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:33.830
These short movies were usually screened in
variety shows or a smorgasbord of short films.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:38.700
You’d drop into a Nickelodeon — a lower-class
theatre where you’d see a bunch of movies

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.500
for a nickel — 5 cents.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:46.500
You’d see Laura Comstock’s Bag Punching
Dog, followed by The Trapeze Disrobing Act,

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.650
followed by the first screen adaptation of
Frankenstein.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:53.470
The whole thing was 14 minutes long - 975
feet.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:59.229
In America, some of this was enforced by East
Coast movie trusts that controlled and licensed

00:01:59.229 --> 00:02:01.430
movie patents for film and projection.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.950
They preferred single and double reel films.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:08.070
But even renegade filmmakers out in Hollywood
kept movies pretty short.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:11.540
And, with some exceptions, so did filmmakers
across Europe.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:16.450
It was just easier to transport and project
one-reel films.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:17.550
That chart, from the beginning?

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.320
It’s not a chart of time, but feet.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.220
And this right here is a thousand feet - that
one reel cutoff.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:27.080
For the most part, movies were...small.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:33.380
Until the Italians thought bigger.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:36.660
Italian movies were were becoming spectacles
in the 1900s.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.970
They were breaking out of the standard 15-minute
short, and trying new things.

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:47.220
By being outside the American system of high-powered
movie trusts, one film in particular was able

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.890
to rewrite the rules.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:56.040
That’s 1913’s Quo Vadis — an epic story
of Romans in the time of Nero.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:01.570
To tell that story the Italian filmmakers
took 2 whole hours - not 15 minutes.

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:03.480
It was 8,000 feet long!

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.810
To get people to see it, Quo Vadis’s promoters
needed to invent a new business model.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:12.920
Instead of trying to force the long movie
into those movie theaters playing a bunch

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:19.660
of one-reel shorts, promoters rented out classy
concert halls for Quo Vadis alone.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:25.400
They did the same in the US, at the Astor
Theatre in New York, and playing the “mammoth

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:30.790
photodrama” everywhere from Arkansas to
the future state of Hawaii.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:35.710
To draw crowds from nickelodeons into those
big halls to watch just one really long thing,

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.920
Quo Vadis needed to be special.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.400
It used real Roman locations, thousands of
extras, and the first big stunts - like chariot

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:49.780
races where the actors rode real chariots
and gladiatorial battles with big hits and

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.480
realistic weapons.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:55.520
The posters sold the spectacle — and sold
out seats.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.920
And people noticed.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:05.920
In the years following the release of Quo
Vadis, DW Griffith, increased the length of

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.400
his own movies.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.800
Quo Vadis and other Italian epics were proof
that big movies could work.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:17.660
Griffith’s movies like “Judith of Bethulia”
crept into feature film lengths - 61 minutes,

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.030
a full 4 reels.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:24.670
He released Birth of a Nation in 1915.

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:29.420
It was sold as the “mightiest spectacle
ever produced” — and a long one clocking

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:30.430
in at 12 reels.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:34.520
It had big stunts too, like many big battle
scenes.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.350
Now this movie was so controversial that people
called it racist in 1915.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:42.250
I mean, the KKK are the good guys.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:46.980
But despite that, it brought the spectacle
of Quo Vadis to Hollywood.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.450
The trusts that wanted to keep movies short
were already fading.

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:53.550
The public’s love of long movies finished
the job.

00:04:53.550 --> 00:05:00.480
Birth of a Nation solidified single movies
as a style worth funding and paying for.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:09.960
And even though today, we don’t measure
movies in feet, Quo Vadis is not a relic.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:16.300
This chart shows average movie times for the
most popular 25 movies each year, from 1930

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.310
on.

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:18.440
This time here?

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:24.710
It’s 2013, 100 years after the 120 minute
Quo Vadis came out.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:29.350
The running time?

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:36.690
121.4 minutes.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:40.110
This edition of Almanac’s all about big
changes to the movies that came from outside

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:44.760
Hollywood, and there are a ton of other business
model changes like Quo Vadis, so let me know

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.930
some of the ones that you’ve heard about
in the comments.

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:53.290
However, I just to just prove to you one more
time that Quo Vadis really was a huge hit.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:06:00.260
This is an ad for “When Ursus Threw the
Bull,” a parody movie that was made in response

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.160
to Quo Vadis’s enormous success.

